Electron beam evaporation is a physical vapor deposition (PVD) technique that uses an electron beam under vacuum to directly heat evaporated material (usually particles) and deliver the evaporated material to a substrate to form a thin film.
The furnace bed housing the evaporating material must therefore be water cooled to avoid melting.
However, electron beam evaporation cannot be used to coat internal surfaces with complex geometries.
E-beam evaporation is used in a wide variety of applications due to its high deposition rates and high material utilisation efficiency.
Electron beam evaporation is also used in optical films, including laser optics, solar panels, glass and architectural glazing to give them the required conductive, reflective and transmissive properties.
Compared to thermal evaporation, electron beam evaporation provides high energy; however, controlling the thickness of the film to the 5nm level will be difficult.
Compared to thermal evaporation, electron beam evaporation offers many advantages
E-beam evaporation can heat the material to a higher temperature than thermal evaporation.
The water cooling of the crucible strictly limits the electron beam heating to the area occupied by the source material only, thus eliminating any unwanted contamination of adjacent components.
Electron beam evaporation sources are available in a variety of sizes and configurations, including single or multiple chambers.
