Learn about this shortcut to unlock Windows without having to restart your computer | Windows News
2 A shortcut to unlock Windows without having to restart your computer
This is when we immediately resort to the keyboard shortcut to reactivate the most popular and well-known computer: CTRL+ALT+Del. What this does is immediately bring up the lock screen with options like opening the Task Manager.
The keyboard shortcuts to wake the system are really interesting, because both the previous one and this one address different problems. Whereas, with Ctlr+Alt+Del the system makes its resources available to the menu with the options to lock, restart and open the Task Manager, the key combination that concerns us today aims at a different objective.
What this shortcut does is reset the system video drivers, so if the hang was caused by problems with them, then it will be fixed without a reboot. In that sense, when you press the key combination, you will hear a beep and you will see the screen go black, but do not worry.
What to do if this shortcut doesn't work?
In general, all CD or DVD-ROM drives have a physical eject button that can be used to open or close the tray. This is usually located next to the door of the tray itself, marked with an icon of an upward-pointing triangle and a horizontal line just below.
If so, it will often be enough to stop or close that application to trigger the eject mechanism again.
3 Intervene in the unit (on a desktop PC)
If the "Windows method" did not work, it is very likely that there has been a jam in the opening and closing mechanism of the tray. If so, you have to resort to "hand method" for which a little skill and a very fine tool are required.
The next step is to locate a small hole next to the opening and closing door on the CD or DVD-ROM drive. is the call "manual release hole", round and tiny, but not so narrow that our fine tool doesn't fit*.
Even if the pressure has been successful and the tray opens, it may not open completely. In this case, we will gently pull (sometimes, the jam occurs because the disc has moved inside the tray) to release the tray and remove it.
Intervene in the unit (on a desktop PC)
First of all, we are going to have to remove the side panel of the computer in order to access the reader. Normally, these panels are attached with small thumb screws, so we will have to use a suitable screwdriver.
