Full Program
Cultural partners work with the festival collaboratively to build the program by contributing their own creative projects.
Work with Us
We believe that everyone’s contribution will help make Our City Festival a truly accessible and dynamic festival of art, architecture and ideas. Generous financial and in-kind support from corporate sponsors, private donors and contributors will help cover or alleviate the costs associated with art production, events, marketing and a small operations team across Phnom Penh, Battambang and Siem Reap.
Volunteer with Us
People who donate a minimum of USD$1,000 will be recognised as Our City Leaders and receive the following in return for their generosity:
• Acknowledgement on the Our City Leaders event honour wall
• Reserved seating at VIP opening
• An exclusively designed festival gift
OCF activates Cambodia’s urban environments through art, architecture and ideas that is characterized by a spirit of accessibility, learning and innovation for all.
In the past five years, it has operated as a creative and critical exploration of the city’s urban trajectory whilst seeking to reflect on change and document them through artistic response and expression.
If you have any questions, comments, enquires or would like to know more about the festival please do not hesitate in contacting us.
The festival dates for all three cities are:
The submission deadline for OCF, 2014 volunteer program has passed. However, OCF is interested in hearing from volunteers with special skills who are available any time between December 2013 and January 2014.
How can I support the festival?
What type of events can we expect?
We offer art and architectural themed:
- Educational talk and tours
Created by selected international and local artists working in the following media formats:
The time and locations of events will be confirmed once the program is finalised and released to the public.
Festival team members and Youth Ambassadors complete child protection training with Friends International and must comply with a child protection agreement available in both Khmer and English.
OCF is organized by JavaArts, a cultural non-profit enterprise that was launched in partnership with Java Cafe & Gallery in 2000 in Phnom Penh, where it operates a gallery and arts lab.
OCF operates as a non-profit with an external Advisory Committee that reviews all process. Currently, it does not have official non-profit status but we are in the process of registering as a 501(c)(3) in the United States of America.
Festival Facts Our City Festival began in 2008 as a response to the shifting urban environment in Phnom Penh
A majority of the festival creatives are locally based with an invitation being extended to international creatives
For OCF 14 we have received over 50 creative applications
OCF 2014 will span across three of Cambodia’s largest cities for the first time thus making it the biggest OCF festival yet! These three cities include Phnom Penh, Battambang and Siem Reap
OCF founded the Youth Ambassador’s program. A volunteer opportunity to help engage and empower young people so they can make a valuable and practical contribution to the festival, learn and have fun along the way
Projects and events are created by selected international and local artists working in the following media format: photography; painting; mixed media; animation; drawing; digital media
OCF was initially developed as an annual festival however following 2014 it will be a biannual festival that works locally in Cambodia
OCF Director and Founder, Dana Langlois has focused on contemporary Cambodian art for more than a decade
Throughout each festival, Our City Festival invites creative, passionate and committed young people to be Youth Ambassadors and participate in our production, marketing and research activities. The Youth Ambassadors Program is a volunteer opportunity to help engage and empower young people so they can make a valuable and practical contribution to the festival, plus learn and have fun along the way.
But what happened when there were no homes left? This project is a study, using the mediums of photography and painting, on the social meanings of public and private space, and the “homestructing” that occurred during this period of Cambodia’s history.
A collaborative project between architects and artists, showcasing new and innovative ideas for the city of Phnom Penh, from the perspective of the city’s residents. The city’s economic expansion has been followed by a period of tremendous construction, with many public buildings and much land being privatized.
A continuation of ‘The Black Wood’ (OCF 2012), ‘Still’ is an exhibition of sculpture, photography and mixed media investigating notions of home and security in the urban environment. The work studies the plight of vulnerable and displaced communities in Phnom Penh.
This work was born from an idea of mixing the magic of a photographic moment with circus performance. But is the magic of the circus related to the specific place and time, or is it independent?
From inside a tuktuk, meet ChildSafe Network Members and learn how they protect children in their daily lives while enjoying a refreshing drink and snack available for sale from community stallholders. Extra shade will be provided by ANZ Royal umbrella.
Cambodia Knits invites the public to discover knitting through a hands on workshop—after a 5 minute lesson, anyone can knit!
Artworks appear among the market stalls, restaurants, hairdressers, shops and storefronts along the ground floor of the White Building, featuring a variety of installation and documentation of performances and a retrospective of work produced by Sa Sa Art Projects and Aziza School. Location: Along the ground floor shopfronts at the White Building.
Join us at the Festival Lounge, the official Our City Festival bar at The Mansion.
Join curator Sovan Philong and participating artists to discover “ideas for our city” and how they were developed, on this special guided tour, held in Khmer.
A parade of musicians riding in cyclos, “yarn-stormed” by Cambodia Knits with the Cyclo Conservation and Careers Association, will ride down Sothearos Blvd and arrive at The Mansion where a group of hip hop dancers from Tiny Toones will be waiting. Together they will form a Flash Mob at 5:30pm, bringing together classical and contemporary forms of music and dance.
MORE DETAILS Ideas for Our City: Phnom Penh (Phnom Penh)
This group exhibition approaches contemporary performance practices through their relation to the city. A 90-minute single-channel screening features twelve videos by nine artists and a reading station offers contextual material from the past and present.
This work explores the connections between people, nature and climate change to help inspire people to create healthier and more natural shared spaces through communal gardens.
A contemporary dance-piece directed by Bob Ruijzendaal on the theme of searching for connection, and in the conceptual sense, falling down and standing up again. Through physical movement and performance, the piece examines the concept of being alone, yet together at the same time.
This photo essay examines various locations in Siem Reap and analyzes their level of accessibility for residents with disabilities. This work was created by six members of the Jesuit Centre with varying levels of mobility.
Light our Lives (Siem Reap) Exhibition
Following a design degree and background in architecture, Daen graduated with a Bachelor of Art History at the University of Queensland. She has worked for galleries such as the Queensland Art Gallery/Gallery of Modern Art and The Institute of Modern Art, Brisbane.
Download the Program details in PDF OCF Khmer Program and OCF English Program
The ancient craft of knitting takes on contemporary expressions through the playful gesture of “yarn-storming.” Cambodia Knits have dressed and covered 6 cyclos in collaboration with the Cyclo Conservation & Career Association, which can be found moving around Phnom Penh and at festival events.
Join curator Sovan Philong and participating artists to discover “ideas for our city” and how they were developed, on this special guided tour, held in English.
MORE DETAILS Photo Exhibition “A Year in the Building” Art Archive (Phnom Penh)
Ideas for Our City: Phnom Penh (Phnom Penh)
From inside a tuktuk, meet ChildSafe Network Members and learn how they protect children in their daily lives while enjoying a refreshing drink and snack available for sale from community stallholders. Extra shade will be provided by ANZ Royal unbrellas.
Pisaot’s artist-in-residence Nguyen Thi Thanh Mai welcomes the public to her studio and shows work in progress at Sa Sa Art Projects. Sa Sa Art Projects, 2nd Floor, between 4th and 5th stairways of the White Building.
An exhibition of designs, models and 3D animations by architecture students that were created in response to a workshop on Design as Activism and Community Upgrading facilitated by Sahmakum Teang Tnaut (STT). The workshop focused on two vulnerable communities: Andong and along the Railroad tracks, and asked the participating students to develop design solutions that are economically accessible for the residents and provide a dignified living.
This community mural draws on themes of connecting people and ideas across spaces through sharing stories, art, music and creating cohesive supportive arts communities in challenged areas.
This ballet emphasises the importance of the cultural and traditional considerations of the Khmer Angkorian temples as living temples and part of contemporary urban life.
Light our Lives (Siem Reap)
The children and electrician apprentices at Grace House Community Centre explore the effects of Siem Reap’s growth on rural life just 4 kilometers outside the city. This installation focuses on the lives of families in the rural villages who struggle to pay for intermittent electricity supply, most who only have access to a light, TV and fan.
A short film featuring timelapse sequences of Siem Reap landmarks and daily scenes. The timelapse technique allows us to see small changes that occur over time and serve as a testimonial of the rapid urbanization of the city.
The Battambang Art Tour by tuk tuk is designed for art lovers, students, collectors, dealers and curators. It will offer insight into the arts community and industry of Battambang via visits to galleries, schools, studios, meet-ups with artists and watching artisans hone their craft.
The Street 2.5 “Artwalk” features an art market with local artists selling crafts and locally produced items, street performances including Khmer dance and Lakhaon, as well as music and performance art. Street vendors and nearby restaurants sell local specialties, while surrounding galleries including Make Maek Art Space and Lotus Galleries are open throughout the evening.
This self-guided tour, running throughout the weekend, offers a unique journey through Battambang featuring icons and symbols which have been created especially by artists during a pre-Festival workshop. The project provides a platform for the wider community to access the city and explore the space they inhabit in non-traditional ways, all with the goal of citizen empowerment and effecting meaningful change on issues raised by urbanization.
This performance work is based on the theme of sickness and social disease, both in an individual and physical sense, as well as its wider environmental and societal forms. Using the symbol of the glass cup (Keo Jup), widely used by Khmer people to cure illness, the artist re-imagines sickness as something to display and expose to the world, rather than a thing of shame to hide behind closed doors.
Amrita Performing Arts is a contemporary Cambodian dance company based in Phnom Penh. For this year’s Our City Festival Amrita invited individual artists, groups and organizations to share existing, or create new, dance films that explored the idea of ‘Our City’.
Get connected with OCF through our social media outlets. Do not forget to use the #OCF14 whenever you post!
Sovan Philong is an independent professional photographer working and living in Phnom Penh. Beginning his professional career in 2009, he worked as a press photographer for The Phnom Penh Post and then later on for the Xinua News Agency.
Recently relocating to Cambodia from France where he studied at Ecole Nationale Supérieure Louis-Lumière through the support of the French Government grant, Philong joins Our City Festival as the City Curator for Phnom Penh.
Sasha has been curating art exhibitions to promote Cambodian contemporary artists since 2002 and also managed the Art House gallery in Siem Reap (2006-2008).
In 2011, Olivia embarked on a cross-cultural professional challenge and relocated to Cambodia with Australian Volunteers International for a fundraising and communications assignment at Child Helpline Cambodia.
Hang Sokunthea is a senior student at Pannasastra University of Cambodia studying Communication and Media Arts with a major in Mass Media and International Relations. Possessing a strong passion in the field of Media Communications, she is currently working as a freelance writer for a local publication and is also an advocate for education, social awareness and development in the community.
To our family of supporters, thank you for making Our City Festival possible!
Our sponsorship packages provide a wealth of high value marketing benefits and bespoke, integrated activation opportunities that can be tailored to meet your branding objectives and budget parameters. We are also delighted to offer VIP access and corporate hospitality.
With an extensive background in Arts and Culture working as an Arts Manager, Creative Producer and Curator for international arts festivals and cultural organizations, Michelle O’Brien joins Our City Festival as the Events Manager.
She has also recently been selected as a participant for European Festivals Association’s 2014 Atelier for Young Festival Managers.
With a background in visual arts, Dana Langlois has focused on contemporary Cambodian art for more than a decade. She curates solo shows of emerging artists and has collaborated with other arts practitioners to produce exhibitions and projects.
She is involved in research and documentation of contemporary arts practices in Cambodia and being based in Phnom Penh since 1998, is one of the more active and long-standing practitioners in this field.
Through out each festival, Our City Festival invites creative, passionate and committed young people to be Youth Ambassadors and participate in our production, marketing and research activities. The Youth Ambassadors Program is a volunteer opportunity to help engage and empower young people so they can make a valuable and practical contribution to the festival, plus learn and have fun along the way.
Alongside his role as Assistant Producer for Our City Festival, he also works as an IT lecturer at the Institute of Technology of Cambodia following his Master of Information Technology studies at INSA de Rennes, France.
